WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.000
The lamp is outside the cells.

2
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.560
How does each cell record where it sits?

3
00:00:08.583 --> 00:00:13.883
Inside the cells, position is recorded as levels of transcription factors, proteins

4
00:00:13.883 --> 00:00:15.803
that switch genes on or off.

5
00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:20.847
Two of them run in opposite directions along the front-to-back axis.

6
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.300
Emx2 is highest at the back.

7
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.580
Pax6 is highest at the front.

8
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:31.843
Each also varies from the midline outward, and two more factors join them.

9
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:36.837
The levels of these proteins are each progenitor's record of where it sits.

10
00:00:37.917 --> 00:00:39.757
The record and the lamp are linked.

11
00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:42.957
Changing FGF8 shifts the gradients.

12
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:46.677
Whether Emx2 acts back on it is still argued.

13
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.070
A gradient alone is only a correlation.

14
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.910
So run both tests: remove the factor, then add more.

15
00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.320
Emx2 is highest at the back.

16
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.320
Delete it. Which way should the borders move, and which areas should grow?

17
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:10.073
The borders move back.

18
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:14.033
The front-and-middle areas grow, and the visual area shrinks.

19
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:20.103
In 2000, Kathie Bishop, Guy Goudreau and Dennis O'Leary found this in mice lacking

20
00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:21.123
Emx2.

21
00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:25.163
Without working Pax6, the shift went the other way.

22
00:01:26.023 --> 00:01:28.703
Front areas shrank, and the visual area grew forward.

23
00:01:29.716 --> 00:01:32.756
Now raise Emx2 everywhere, keeping its slope.

24
00:01:33.616 --> 00:01:35.176
What would show that it allocates territory?

25
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.020
The map should move the opposite way, with the whole cortex about the same size.

26
00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:52.393
In 2004, Tadashi Hamasaki and colleagues in O'Leary's laboratory found this.

27
00:01:53.316 --> 00:01:56.216
The visual area grew and the front areas shrank.

28
00:01:57.136 --> 00:01:59.676
Total cortical area did not change significantly.

29
00:02:00.816 --> 00:02:04.116
Loss and gain move the borders in opposite directions.

30
00:02:04.956 --> 00:02:09.596
That is strong evidence for allocating territory, not simply permitting cortex to

31
00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:10.596
exist.

32
00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:13.886
COUP-TFI tells a similar story for loss.

33
00:02:14.916 --> 00:02:19.856
In 2007, Michèle Studer's laboratory removed it from cortex alone.

34
00:02:20.916 --> 00:02:25.576
Frontal motor areas spread across most of the sheet, and the sensory areas were

35
00:02:25.576 --> 00:02:26.736
squeezed toward the back.

36
00:02:27.716 --> 00:02:29.836
Pax6 is the cautionary case.

37
00:02:30.656 --> 00:02:34.956
In 2007, David Price's group raised it up to three times normal.

38
00:02:35.999 --> 00:02:37.259
The map barely moved.

39
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:41.253
A knockout limited to cortex shifted the markers.

40
00:02:41.773 --> 00:02:45.473
Yet the area-specific connections with the thalamus stayed normal.

41
00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:51.613
So Pax6 may bias how markers are expressed more than it decides where an area sits.

42
00:02:52.473 --> 00:02:56.233
But in 2013, a cortex-only knockout shrank the touch area.

43
00:02:57.093 --> 00:02:58.533
Its role is still unsettled.

44
00:02:59.416 --> 00:03:03.856
The takeaway: when losing a factor and adding it move borders in opposite

45
00:03:03.856 --> 00:03:08.536
directions, that is the strongest evidence the factor helps allocate territory.

46
00:03:09.436 --> 00:03:12.356
A missing converse result does not rule a factor out.
